Here’s a **quick cheat sheet** you can keep in mind 📘:

---

## ✅ Common **Semantic Tags**

These tags clearly define the role/meaning of the content.

* **Headings & Text**:

  * `<h1>` to `<h6>` → headings (importance hierarchy)
  * `<p>` → paragraph
  * `<strong>` → strong importance (usually bold)
  * `<em>` → emphasized text (usually italic)
  * `<blockquote>` → block quotation
  * `<cite>` → citation

* **Structure & Layout**:

  * `<header>` → intro/header of page/section
  * `<footer>` → footer of page/section
  * `<main>` → main content of a page
  * `<article>` → independent content (e.g., blog post, news article)
  * `<section>` → thematic grouping of content
  * `<nav>` → navigation links
  * `<aside>` → side content (e.g., sidebar, ads)

* **Lists & Media**:

  * `<ul>` → unordered list
  * `<ol>` → ordered list
  * `<li>` → list item
  * `<figure>` → self-contained content (image, chart, etc.)
  * `<figcaption>` → caption for figure
  * `<img>` → image (semantic with `alt`)
  * `<audio>` / `<video>` → media elements
<a>
* **Tables (for actual tabular data)**:

  * `<table>`
  * `<thead>`, `<tbody>`, `<tfoot>`
  * `<tr>`, `<th>`, `<td>`
  * `<caption>`

---

## ❌ Common **Non-Semantic Tags**

These do **not** provide meaning, just generic containers or inline styling.

* `<div>` → generic block container
* `<span>` → generic inline container
* `<b>` → bold (visual only, not semantic; use `<strong>` instead if meaning is needed)
* `<i>` → italic (visual only, not semantic; use `<em>` instead if meaning is needed)
* `<font>` → deprecated (use CSS instead)
* `<center>` → deprecated (use CSS instead)

---

👉 Rule of thumb:

* If a tag tells *what the content is*, it’s **semantic**.
* If a tag is just for grouping or styling, it’s **non-semantic**.

