## 1️⃣ Block-level vs Inline-level Elements

### 🔹 Block-level elements

* Always **start on a new line**.
* Take up the **full width** available (stretch across the container).
* Can contain **other block or inline elements**.
* Examples: `<div>`, `<p>`, `<h1>`–`<h6>`, `<ul>`, `<ol>`, `<li>`, `<section>`, `<article>` nav footer

✔️ Think of block-level elements as **big building blocks** of a page.

---

### 🔹 Inline elements

* Do **not start on a new line**; they flow **within a line of text**.
* Take up only as much **width as their content**.
* Can only contain **text or other inline elements** (not block-level).
* Examples: `<span>`, `<a>`, `<strong>`, `<em>`, `<img>`, `<label>`

✔️ Think of inline elements as **small pieces of content inside a line**.

---

💡 Example:

```html
<p>This is a <strong>block</strong> and <span>inline</span> example.</p>
```

* `<p>` is **block-level** (new line).
* `<strong>` and `<span>` are **inline** (stay inside the sentence).

---

## 2️⃣ Difference between **Element** and **Tag**

### 🔹 Tag

* A **piece of code enclosed in angle brackets** (`< >`).
* Can be **opening tag** (`<p>`) or **closing tag** (`</p>`).
* Example: `<h1>` is a tag.

---

### 🔹 Element

* The **complete structure**, including opening tag, content, and closing tag.
* Example:

```html
<h1>Hello World</h1>
```

* `<h1>` = opening tag
* `Hello World` = content
* `</h1>` = closing tag
* Together they form an **element**: `<h1>Hello World</h1>`

---

👉 So:

* **Tag** = the keyword inside `< >`.
* **Element** = the whole thing (opening tag + content + closing tag).

