Meta tags
What are Meta Tags in HTML?

Meta tags are tiny elements of HTML that live inside the <head> section of a webpage.
They provide metadata (data about data) — information about the webpage, not shown directly on the page(browser/screen).
They help browsers, search engines, and social media platforms understand your webpage better.

Common Uses of Meta Tags
1. SEO (Search Engine Optimization)

Meta tags tell search engines what the page is about.
Example:

<meta name="description" content="This is a blog about fitness and nutrition.">
<meta name="keywords" content="fitness, gym, nutrition, weight loss">


2-When you share a link on Facebook, Twitter, WhatsApp, etc., the preview info comes from meta tags.
Example:

<meta property="og:title" content="My Fitness Blog">
<meta property="og:description" content="Get tips on workouts, nutrition, and health.">
<meta property="og:image" content="https://example.com/blog-image.jpg">


og:title, og:description, and og:image → control what appears in the preview card.

3-Author & Copyright Info
<meta name="author" content="John Doe">
<meta name="copyright" content="2025 MyCompany">

-4-Control Page Behavior

You can control how browsers render the page.
Example:

<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">


charset="UTF-8" → Ensures correct text encoding (so all languages/symbols display properly).

viewport → Makes web pages responsive (important for mobile).



